* fork.i -- the two-process harness for CONF68K.** Every claim about record locking is a claim about what ONE process sees* while ANOTHER holds something. The eighteen tests that came first are all* single-process, and no amount of care makes a single process able to* observe a lock: the holder never conflicts with itself. RBF says so* explicitly -- "RBF notices that the same process owns both paths and keeps* them from locking each other out" (Technical Manual, page 7-8) -- so even* opening the same file twice from one process proves nothing.** This file is what those tests were missing. It is pulled in with* "use fork.i" AFTER the body and AFTER verdict.i, and before report.i.* That position is not arbitrary: verdict.i has to stay adjacent to the body* so the body's short "beq.s ispass" branches still reach it, and everything* here is reached by word-sized bsr instead.** ONE MODULE, TWO PROCESSES** A test built on this runs twice, as two processes, from ONE module. The* parent forks the very same module again and passes it a one-byte parameter* "C"; ckrole reads that parameter and sends the second instance down the* child path. There is no separate helper module to ship, to copy onto an* RBF image, or to keep in step with its parent -- and no way for a test to* find its own helper missing, which is a failure that would otherwise be* reported as a divergence in the behaviour being measured.** WHY THIS IS NOT JUST "FORK AND WAIT"** The obvious shape -- parent takes a lock, forks, waits; child tries the* access and reports what it got -- deadlocks, and the way it deadlocks is* instructive. RBF's documented default is to sleep forever on a locked* record: "Normally, if a read or write request is issued for a part of a* file that is locked out by another user, RBF sleeps indefinitely until the* conflict is removed" (I$SetStt, SS_Ticks). So the child blocks, and the* parent is inside F$Wait and will not release anything until the child* dies. Neither ever moves.** The documented escape is SS_Ticks: "You can use SS_Ticks to return an* error (E$Lock) to the user program if the conflict still exists after the* specified number of ticks have elapsed... A value of one means that if the* record is not released immediately, an error is returned." Every child* here asks for exactly that. But a suite that RELIES on it can only run on* a system that already implements it correctly, and this one does not:* os9exec accepts SS_Ticks, stores the value, and never applies it to a* blocked writer, so the child hangs anyway (recorded in* DOCS/known-divergences). A conformance test that hangs reports nothing at* all, takes the rest of the run down with it, and is strictly worse than* one that fails.** So the parent does not trust the timeout. It rescues the child* unconditionally:**   parent                              child*   ------                              -----*   take the lock the claim is about*   fork ------------------------------> open the file, ask for SS_Ticks 1*   wait for SCRATCH/ready               position for the contended access*      <-------------------------------- create SCRATCH/ready*   sleep RELDELAY ticks                 perform the contended access*   create SCRATCH/freed                    (returns at once, or blocks)*   release every lock held on d7 -----> ... and now it cannot still block*   F$Wait                               look for SCRATCH/freed, report** The release is unconditional and time-bounded, so whatever the child was* waiting for is free before the parent ever waits on the child. The pair* cannot deadlock on any system, including one where SS_Ticks does nothing.** WHAT THE CHILD REPORTS, AND WHY "IT SUCCEEDED" IS NOT ONE ANSWER** Rescuing the child costs the test its simplest reading. "The write* succeeded" now covers two opposite worlds: there was no lock in force at* all, or there was one and the child sat behind it until the parent let go.* A test that could not tell those apart would pass on a system with no* record locking whatsoever, which is the single most important thing it* must not do.** SCRATCH/freed is what separates them. The parent creates it BEFORE it* releases, so the child -- which looks only after its access has returned* -- finds it present exactly when its access outlived the parent's hold.* Three outcomes, all distinguishable:**   an error code   the access was refused; the code is the observation*                   (252, E$Lock, is the conforming answer where a*                   conflict was expected)*   BLOCKED (901)   the access succeeded, but only after the holder let go:*                   the lock was real and the declared timeout was ignored*   0               the access succeeded while the lock was still held:*                   there was no effective lock** Neither 900 nor 901 is an OS-9 error code, so neither can be confused* with one.** THE RESIDUAL RACE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN** The handshake marks the moment the child is ABOUT to make its contended* call, not the moment it blocks -- nothing a process can do observes its own* impending sleep. If the child were descheduled between creating* SCRATCH/ready and issuing the call, and stayed descheduled for the whole* of RELDELAY, it would make its call after the release and report BLOCKED* on a system that would have answered E$Lock. RELDELAY is set far above the* handful of instructions in that gap for exactly this reason. It is a real* if remote possibility and it is recorded in claims.md rather than being* left for a reader to discover from a flaky result.*FFork set $03FWait set $04FSend set $08FExit set $06FSleep set $0AIOpenF set $84ICreatF set $83IDeletF set $87ICloseF set $8FISetStt set $8ESSTicks set $10SSLock set $11FATTRS set $001B** Reasonable-looking parameter sizes only. The child's marker is one byte,* and the shell passes a bare carriage return when a command has no* arguments, so anything past a line's worth means the entry registers are* not what this code thinks they are and (a5) must not be dereferenced.PARMMAX set 256** Reported when F$Wait kept waking us for something that was not our child.* Deliberately below the 1000 band and outside the OS-9 error range, so an* ERROR line carrying it cannot be mistaken for a system error code.SPNOWAIT set 900** The child's access succeeded, but not until the holder released. Same* number as SPNOWAIT is deliberately avoided: this one is an OBSERVATION and* appears on PASS/FAIL lines, so it must not collide with a code that only* ever appears on ERROR lines.BLOCKED set 901** The child never came back at all: it was still inside its contended call* long after the holder released everything, so nothing it could have been* waiting for still existed. The parent kills it and reports this rather* than waiting on it, because a conformance test that hangs reports nothing* and takes every later test with it.HUNG set 902** How long the parent lets the child sit on its contended call before* rescuing it. Ticks. Long enough that a system honouring SS_Ticks 1 has* answered many times over, short enough that eight tests do not add up to a* timeout.RELDELAY set 60** How long the parent waits for the child to say it has finished, after the* release that must have unblocked it. Ticks. Running out means the child is* wedged in a call that never returned, which is an observation about the* system and not a fault in the test -- see HUNG.DONEMAX set 400** How many times the parent looks for the child's ready marker, sleeping one* tick between tries, before giving up and rescuing anyway. A child that* never gets that far ends in the 2000 band and the test reports ERROR, so* running out here costs a verdict but never the run.READYMAX set 600** ckrole -- decide whether this instance is the parent or the forked child.** Reads the entry registers d5 (parameter size) and a5 (parameter pointer),* so a test MUST call it before anything else clobbers them -- it is the* first instruction of every two-process test for that reason.** Returns d0.w = 1 for the child, 0 for the parent, and is deliberately* biased towards "parent": a5 is only dereferenced once d5 has been checked* to be both non-zero and small. Under --noshell a test is booted directly* rather than forked by a shell and the parameter area is empty; reading* (a5) on the strength of an undefined d5 would fault there instead of* running the test.ckrole moveq #0,d0 tst.l d5 beq.s ckrdone cmpi.l #PARMMAX,d5 bhi.s ckrdone cmpi.b #'C',(a5) bne.s ckrdone moveq #1,d0ckrdone rts** canlock -- can this device do record locking at all? Takes nothing.**   carry set    d1 = the code that said no; the caller reports SKIP*   carry clear  locking is available and the test can give a verdict** The probe is SS_Lock with a size of zero, which the manual defines as "If* 0 bytes are requested, all locks are removed (Record Lock, EOF Lock, and* File Lock)". Releasing locks a fresh path does not hold is the one form of* the call with no side effect whatsoever, so probing cannot disturb the* thing being measured -- and a device with no record locking under it has* no SS_Lock to answer with and says so.** Why a test needs this at all: these are RBF claims, and RBF is a disk file* manager. os9exec can also present a plain host directory under the same* device name, which is a convenience shim with no allocation map, no file* descriptors and no locks. Every claim here would pass or fail there by* accident, and neither outcome would say anything about OS-9. SKIP is the* honest verdict, and it is why the suite's report has had a SKIP column* since before any test could produce one.** NOT SS_Ticks, which is the other RBF record-locking SetStat and the more* obvious probe. On this emulator SS_Ticks on a non-RBF path is a wild jump* through an uninitialised dispatch slot and kills the caller with a bus* error, so probing with it would crash exactly the runs it exists to skip.* That is a defect in the emulator rather than anything about OS-9, and it is* recorded in DOCS/known-divergences; the probe avoids it rather than* depending on it being fixed. On a real system either call would do.* It runs on a scratch file of its own rather than on the caller's path, and* that is not tidiness. SS_Lock(0) is a RELEASE -- of "Record Lock, EOF Lock,* and File Lock" -- so probing with it on the path under test would destroy* the very lock the test had just taken. The EOF-lock tests are where that* bites, because a file's creator holds EOF lock from the moment it is* created and there is no point at which the path is both open and unlocked.* A separate file has no such problem and answers the same question, since* what is being asked about is the DEVICE.canlock movem.l d0-d2/d7/a0,-(a7) lea prbmark(pc),a0 bsr rmmark lea prbmark(pc),a0 moveq #2,d0 move.w #FATTRS,d1 clr.l d2 trap #0 dc.w ICreatF bcs.s cluerr move.w d0,d7 move.w #SSLock,d1 moveq #0,d2 trap #0 dc.w ISetStt bcs.s clufail moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 trap #0 dc.w ICloseF lea prbmark(pc),a0 bsr rmmark movem.l (a7)+,d0-d2/d7/a0 andi #$FE,ccr rtsclufail move.w d1,d3 moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 trap #0 dc.w ICloseF lea prbmark(pc),a0 bsr rmmark movem.l (a7)+,d0-d2/d7/a0 move.w d3,d1 ori #1,ccr rtscluerr move.w d1,d3 movem.l (a7)+,d0-d2/d7/a0 move.w d3,d1 ori #1,ccr rts** mkmark -- a0 = marker pathname. Create it and close it; a marker's whole* content is whether it exists. An existing marker is not an error, because* the parent clears both markers before it forks and a leftover could only* come from this same run.mkmark move.l a0,-(a7) moveq #2,d0 move.w #FATTRS,d1 clr.l d2 trap #0 dc.w ICreatF bcs.s mkmdone trap #0 dc.w ICloseFmkmdone movea.l (a7)+,a0 rts** ckmark -- a0 = marker pathname.*   carry clear  the marker exists*   carry set    it does notckmark move.l a0,-(a7) moveq #1,d0 trap #0 dc.w IOpenF bcs.s ckmno trap #0 dc.w ICloseF andi #$FE,ccr bra.s ckmdoneckmno ori #1,ccrckmdone movea.l (a7)+,a0 rts** rmmark -- a0 = marker pathname. Delete it, ignoring "was not there".rmmark move.l a0,-(a7) moveq #3,d0 trap #0 dc.w IDeletF movea.l (a7)+,a0 rts** napticks -- d0.l = ticks to sleep.napticks move.l d0,d1 trap #0 dc.w FSleep rts** contend -- d7.w = the open path on which the parent holds whatever lock the claim is about.** Runs the whole protocol drawn at the top of this file and brings back what* the child observed. The caller uses it exactly like a system call, because* it reports failure the same way every other step in these tests does:**   carry set    d1 = a code to report; the body branches to setuperr*   carry clear  d6 = the value the child observed** so a test body reads "bsr contend / bcs.w setuperr" and then compares d6* against EXPECT, with no special handling for the two-process part at all.** The module name is the caller's own psect name. F$Fork looks in the system* module directory before it looks on disk (page 1-30), and the caller is by* definition already resident, so the child links against the copy in memory* and never touches the file system to find itself.contend movem.l d2-d5/a1-a3,-(a7) lea rdymark(pc),a0 bsr rmmark lea frdmark(pc),a0 bsr rmmark lea dnmark(pc),a0 bsr rmmark* moveq #0,d0 moveq #0,d1 moveq #1,d2 moveq #3,d3 moveq #0,d4 lea childpm(pc),a1 lea myname(pc),a0 trap #0 dc.w FFork bcs.w cnforked move.w d0,d5** Wait for the child to say it is about to make its contended call. Bounded:* a child that never arrives must cost this test its verdict, never the run. move.l #READYMAX,d3cnrdy lea rdymark(pc),a0 bsr ckmark bcc.s cngo moveq #1,d0 bsr napticks subq.l #1,d3 bne.s cnrdy** Give the child time to actually block, then rescue it: mark first, release* second, so the marker is already there for any child the release wakes.cngo move.l #RELDELAY,d0 bsr napticks lea frdmark(pc),a0 bsr mkmark moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 move.w #SSLock,d1 moveq #0,d2 trap #0 dc.w ISetStt** The release above freed everything this process held, so a child that is* still inside its contended call is not waiting for anything that exists.* Wait a bounded time for it to say so, and if it never does, kill it and* report HUNG: on a system where a blocked writer is never resumed, this is* the only way the test can report at all rather than wedging the run. move.l #DONEMAX,d3cndone lea dnmark(pc),a0 bsr ckmark bcc.s cnreap moveq #1,d0 bsr napticks subq.l #1,d3 bne.s cndone** Signal 0 is the one the manual guarantees: "This signal terminates the* receiving process, regardless of the state of its signal mask, and is not* intercepted by the intercept handler." move.w d5,d0 moveq #0,d1 trap #0 dc.w FSend moveq #20,d4cnhwait trap #0 dc.w FWait bcs.s cnhung cmp.w d5,d0 beq.s cnhung subq.w #1,d4 bne.s cnhwaitcnhung movem.l (a7)+,d2-d5/a1-a3 move.l #HUNG,d6 andi #$FE,ccr rts** Bounded, because F$Wait is documented to return early on a signal with* "d0.w contains zero, since no child process has terminated" (page 1-71).* Retrying forever on that would turn a stray signal into the hang this* whole file exists to prevent.cnreap moveq #20,d4cnwait trap #0 dc.w FWait bcs.s cnforked cmp.w d5,d0 beq.s cngot subq.w #1,d4 bne.s cnwait movem.l (a7)+,d2-d5/a1-a3 move.w #SPNOWAIT,d1 bra.s cnbad** d1.w is the child's exit status. Decode the bands: only the 1000 band is* an observation, and everything else is the test failing to run rather than* the system failing to conform.cngot movem.l (a7)+,d2-d5/a1-a3 moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6 cmpi.l #2000,d6 blt.s cnobs sub.l #2000,d6 move.w d6,d1 bra.s cnbadcnobs cmpi.l #1000,d6 blt.s cndied sub.l #1000,d6 andi #$FE,ccr rtscndied move.w d6,d1 bra.s cnbad** The fork or the wait itself failed; d1 already carries the system's error.cnforked movem.l (a7)+,d2-d5/a1-a3cnbad ori #1,ccr rts** isskip -- this device cannot exercise the claim at all, so the test has no* verdict to give and says so instead of inventing one. d6 carries the code* that said so.** It lives here rather than in verdict.i on purpose. verdict.i is included by* all eighteen single-process tests, none of which define a skipstr, so* putting a reference to one there would stop every one of them assembling.* Only a test that includes fork.i can reach this, and only such a test needs* it -- record locking is the one area with a device type that cannot run it.isskip lea skipstr(pc),a5 bra.w report** ---- the child's side ----** chticks -- d7.w = path number. Ask RBF to give up rather than sleep, per* the SS_Ticks entry: a delay interval of one returns E$Lock immediately if* the record is not free. The child asks for this even though the parent's* rescue no longer depends on it, because whether it is honoured is exactly* what several of these tests are for.**   carry set    d1 = error; this device does not honour SS_Ticks*   carry clear  the path will time out instead of blocking, if the system*                does what its manual sayschticks moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 move.w #SSTicks,d1 moveq #1,d2 trap #0 dc.w ISetStt rts** chready -- announce that the contended call is the very next thing this* process will do. Everything the child needs must already be open and* positioned before this is called: the parent starts its release clock* here, so any work left between this and the call is time the parent* spends thinking the child is blocked when it is not.chready lea rdymark(pc),a0 bsr mkmark rts** chsaw -- d6.w = what the contended access returned. Turn it into the value* the parent will report, distinguishing "succeeded because nothing was* locked" from "succeeded because the holder finally let go".** Only a success needs deciding; an error carries its own meaning and is* passed through untouched.chsaw tst.w d6 bne.s chsdone lea frdmark(pc),a0 bsr ckmark bcs.s chsdone move.w #BLOCKED,d6chsdone rts** chdone -- d6.w = the value decided by chsaw. Ends the child in the 1000* band: an observation the parent can trust.** The done marker goes down before the exit, and both exits set it, so the* parent can tell "finished, with something to say" from "still wedged"* without ever having to block on F$Wait to find out.chdone move.l d6,-(a7) lea dnmark(pc),a0 bsr mkmark move.l (a7)+,d6 moveq #0,d1 move.w d6,d1 add.l #1000,d1 trap #0 dc.w FExit** chfail -- d1.w = the error that stopped the child before it could contend.* Ends the child in the 2000 band, which the parent turns into ERROR. A child* that cannot open the file has no standing to report on locking, and saying* so is the difference between an honest ERROR and a fabricated PASS.chfail move.l d1,-(a7) lea dnmark(pc),a0 bsr mkmark move.l (a7)+,d1 andi.l #$FFFF,d1 add.l #2000,d1 trap #0 dc.w FExit** The parameter that tells the second instance which half of the test it is.* One byte, and a carriage return after it because that is what the shell* appends to a real command line (page 1-30).* These four are the last thing in this file, and report.i's CODE follows* them, so their total length must stay EVEN or every branch in report.i* lands on an odd address and the assembler rejects the lot. r68 has no* align directive to fall back on -- this was measured, not assumed.** Each one is even on its own rather than only in total, which is what keeps* the rule from being quietly broken by a later edit: the three marker names* are all thirteen characters plus a terminator, and the parameter is two* bytes. Adding a fifth is safe as long as it obeys the same rule.childpm dc.b 'C',13rdymark dc.b "SCRATCH/ready",0prbmark dc.b "SCRATCH/probe",0dnmark dc.b "SCRATCH/ended",0frdmark dc.b "SCRATCH/freed",0